
  <sn><md>## Pain

![img](https://api.hub.jhu.edu/factory/sites/default/files/styles/landscape/public/depression-hub.jpg)

Pain is one of the unavoidable conditions of human existence. From the moment we become conscious of ourselves and the world around us, we encounter loss, failure, uncertainty, physical suffering, disappointment, and fear.

From a religious perspective, suffering can be understood as part of the human condition. In the story of Eden, humanity is separated from paradise as a consequence of disobedience. Whether one interprets this literally, spiritually, or metaphorically, the idea remains powerful: **human life is not designed to be free from suffering.**

We cannot simply blame Adam, our ancestor, for our suffering. Every generation creates its own mistakes, and human beings continue to make choices that produce suffering for themselves and others. Therefore, pain is not merely something inherited from the past; it is also something produced by the way we understand reality and respond to it.

However, **not all pain is the same**. Some pain comes from reality itself, while some pain is created by our expectations, assumptions, and illusions.

